# =====================================================================================
# Sankofa School Platform — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored and must never be committed.
# Every value here is a DUMMY. Nothing in this file is, or has ever been, a real credential.
#
# Each environment (development, preview, production) gets its OWN values. Sharing a
# credential across environments means a preview compromise is a production compromise.
# =====================================================================================

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Supabase (PostgreSQL)
#
# TWO URLs, and the difference matters:
#   DATABASE_URL  Supavisor in TRANSACTION mode, port 6543. Used by the application at
#                 runtime. Serverless functions open and close connections constantly, and
#                 a pooler is what stops that exhausting the connection limit.
#   DIRECT_URL    Supavisor in SESSION mode, port 5432. Used only by `prisma migrate`.
#                 Migrations issue statements a transaction pooler cannot carry.
#
# BOTH LIVE ON THE POOLER HOST — never on db.<project-ref>.supabase.co. That host is
# IPv6-only unless the project buys the IPv4 add-on, and an IPv4-only network (most
# connections in Ghana, and every GitHub-hosted Actions runner) cannot reach it at all.
# `prisma migrate deploy` then fails with P1001, and nothing in the message mentions
# addressing, so it reads as an outage or a wrong password.
#
# COPY BOTH STRINGS FROM THE DASHBOARD: Connect -> ORMs -> Prisma. The hostname is not
# derivable from the region — newer projects land on `aws-1-<region>`, older ones on
# `aws-0-<region>` — and a guessed prefix gives a DNS failure that looks exactly like the
# IPv6 problem above.
#
# The username is `<role>.<project-ref>` on both pooler ports. Drop the suffix and
# Supavisor cannot tell which project you mean: "Tenant or user not found".
#
# `pgbouncer=true` is NOT OPTIONAL on 6543. It disables Prisma's prepared statements,
# which Supavisor in transaction mode does not support. Omit it and the first query under
# any concurrency fails with: prepared statement "s0" already exists. Worse, the error
# persists after you add the flag until the project is restarted — so it reads as though
# the fix did not work. It must NOT be set on DIRECT_URL: migrations need prepared
# statements, and session mode supports them.
#
# `sslmode=require` encrypts but does not verify the server certificate. Prisma's
# PostgreSQL connector supports only prefer, disable and require, so verify-full is not
# available to us. Enforce SSL on the project itself (Database Settings -> SSL
# Configuration); that is the control a client cannot downgrade.
#
# ON THE ROLE: these use `postgres`, which carries BYPASSRLS. That is currently correct
# and deliberate — migration 20260919092000 puts deny-all row-level security on every
# table, and only a bypassing role can still read them. It also means RLS here protects
# the Supabase Data API and NOT this application's own queries. Moving to a dedicated
# non-bypassing role is Phase 2, and it cannot happen before per-tenant policies exist or
# every query in the product returns nothing. See IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md.
# Supabase's own Prisma guide tells you to create a role with `bypassrls`. When Phase 2
# arrives, do not follow it.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL="postgres://postgres.abcdefghijklmnopqrst:EXAMPLE-NOT-A-REAL-PASSWORD@aws-1-eu-west-2.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1&pool_timeout=20&connect_timeout=15&sslmode=require"
DIRECT_URL="postgres://postgres.abcdefghijklmnopqrst:EXAMPLE-NOT-A-REAL-PASSWORD@aws-1-eu-west-2.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require"

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
#
# NEXTAUTH_SECRET signs the session token. 32+ random bytes, different per environment:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# NEXTAUTH_URL is the canonical URL of the deployment. On Vercel production this is your
# domain; leave it unset in preview deployments and Vercel's own URL is used.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXTAUTH_SECRET="change-me-generate-with-openssl-rand-base64-48"
NEXTAUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email — used for invitations and password resets
#
# NOT WIRED UP YET. Nothing sends email, so these values are currently unused. They are
# listed so the shape is known and so nobody discovers the requirement at deploy time.
# See IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESEND_API_KEY=""
EMAIL_FROM="Sankofa <no-reply@example.com>"

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMS — Ghana providers. Not wired up yet; listed so the shape is known.
#
# SMS costs real money per message, so quotas and a bulk-send confirmation gate are
# enforced in the application, not by the provider.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MNOTIFY_API_KEY=""
MNOTIFY_SENDER_ID=""

# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="http://localhost:3000"
